## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical IPC with shared memory and demonstrates mastery of memory ordering in a real, implementable primitive.

## Key Concepts
- Single-producer/single-consumer lock-free design
- Atomic operations and acquire/release semantics
- Memory visibility across cores and cache-line padding
- mmap-based IPC and data layout in shared memory

## Code Example
```c
// Simplified sketch (not production-ready)
typedef struct {
  uint64_t head; // read index
  uint64_t tail; // write index
  char data[4096];
} ring_t;

void prod(ring_t *r, const void *src, size_t n){
  uint64_t t = __atomic_load_n(&r->tail, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  // write data to slot (omitting slot indexing details)
  memcpy(r->data + (t % 1024) * 4, src, n);
  __atomic_store_n(&r->tail, t+1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
}

void cons(ring_t *r, void *dst, size_t n){
  uint64_t t = __atomic_load_n(&r->tail, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE);
  uint64_t h = __atomic_load_n(&r->head, __ATOMIC_RELAXED);
  if (h < t){
    memcpy(dst, r->data + (h % 1024) * 4, n);
    __atomic_store_n(&r->head, h+1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for multiple producers? discuss ring partitioning or a per-slot sequence counter.
- What changes for varying message sizes or overflow handling? discuss padding and data-copy costs.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of memory ordering, lock-free data structures, and weak memory models in OS CPUs. It probes ability to identify a real race and reason about inter-thread visibility across architectures.

## Key Concepts

- Lock-free synchronization
- memory_order_release
- memory_order_acquire
- Data visibility before signaling availability

## Code Example

```cpp
// Pseudo-C++ lock-free sketch
std::atomic<size_t> head{0}, tail{0};
int data[N];

void produce(int v){
  auto t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  data[t % N] = v;
  tail.store((t+1) % N, std::memory_order_release);
}

int consume(){
  auto t = tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire);
  auto h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
  if (h == t) return -1;
  int v = data[h % N];
  head.store((h+1) % N, std::memory_order_release);
  return v;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this pattern for multiple producers/consumers?
- What tests would you add to validate correctness across ARM/x86?

